(objection 'o-kernel-canon-1 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'd-kernel-app-door :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'frontend-is-ui-only "The decision promises that a program never holds keys, never changes a standing rule and only signs inside the face's limits, and it says where that is checked: in the kernel, which the same decision places in the program the person runs. The runtime's own connection has two levels, read-only and full, so nothing below the kernel stops a trading program that was given the connection from signing anything. The runtime owns logic and the frontend only shows and asks, so a limit that only the frontend keeps is a request, not a limit, and a person who sees 'signed inside your limits' believes more than the system holds. no-stubs-in-flows forbids a hidden compromise, and this is one unless the person is told on the face that the limit is not enforced below the interface. The decision already names the maintainer as decider and lists host-enforced scopes as work not done, but its leaning ships the unenforced version as the launch answer and k-default-no-programs, k-app-door and app-trading-program describe it as a guarantee. This needs a maintainer decision on whether apps that sign may launch before the scopes exist; none exists in this file."))

(objection 'o-kernel-canon-2 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'k-leave-flow :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'no-real-funds-before-dispute-fix "Every face's remedy against a silent counterparty is this one flow, and it signs exit-prepare, exit-start and exit-finish. The release queue says no real funds before the dispute-replay contract fix lands. The kernel never mentions it: k-real-or-test lists the watching service and routed payments as the protections that differ per chain, not the contract the leaving rests on, and k-leaving-sums promises what leaving 'would return for sure' on every chain. The design quietly assumes the fix lands, so on a chain with the old contract a person is told their secured share comes back when the leaving can be replayed against them. The cost is real money placed on a promise of recovery that does not hold. The right place is the kernel, since all four faces inherit it, and it needs a maintainer decision on the contract fix; no decision in kernel.scm holds it."))

(objection 'o-kernel-canon-3 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'k-coverage :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'recovery-coverage-visible "The release queue gates public launch on an account-level recovery coverage view that shows whether the watching service accepted or failed each job. This kernel screen is the one place all four faces get that answer, and it declares :shows [] and :offers []. k-evidence says the kernel will state 'covered', 'not covered' and accepted or failed, but no figure on any screen carries it, so the checker passes while the person sees a heading and nothing under it. Under errors-are-shown, a failed copy that reaches no one is a swallowed failure. The cost is a person who believes they can leave alone after losing a device when the last copy never arrived. The same emptiness is on k-programs, where the list of what each program caused is promised and not shown."))

(objection 'o-kernel-canon-4 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'd-kernel-evidence :lens "canon"
  :claim (protocol "core/watchtower/submission-support.ts" "The watching service refuses to submit on native TRON, and native TRON is a required launch chain. The decision's honest answer is to say 'not covered' there, and mainnet-open-participation says recovery gates stay mandatory and may not be skipped or hidden by a surface. The decision does not say whether 'not covered' is only a label or a condition: whether a person may put real money, or take a loan, on a chain where no copy can be acted on. If it is only a label, the kernel has chosen to let a recovery gate become information. If it is a condition, that is a gate the wallet invents, which the same rule forbids. Either way this is the maintainer's call, the decider here is already the maintainer, but neither the options nor changes-if state the question, so it can be settled by silence."))

(objection 'o-kernel-canon-5 :by "sonnet-canon" :to 'app-borrowing :lens "canon"
  :claim (canon 'lending-reopened "Lending must appear with exact money, authority, deadlines and recovery. This app takes the whole borrowing side of the launch flow out of the surfaces, and its :through list has money (k-holdings), the question and deadlines (k-attention) but not k-leaving-sums, k-evidence or k-silence, so a person who borrows is never shown what leaving a hub that stops answering would return, or whether a copy of the loan's last state is kept. launch-flows says a spec with several surfaces must say which surface carries each flow on each chain, and neither d-apps-borrowing nor this app says whether borrowing runs on native TRON, where k-evidence says there is no coverage. The cost is a person who owes a hub with no recovery view on the one chain where recovery is weakest. The decision's changes-if covers the maintainer moving the flow, not this gap."))
